  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nMid-level shortwave trough axis is pushing into the eastern Dakotas \nthis afternoon, with the best area of lift and associated rainfall \nnow across the eastern CWA. Continue to make adjustments to PoPs to \nreflect radar trends. Cold front is also making its way eastward \nacross central SD and will be in the James River valley later this \nafternoon. Quick clearing noted behind the departing wave so will \nmake some adjustments to sky cover for the rest of the afternoon. \nNorthwest winds are beginning to increase as expected over central \nSD in the post-frontal atmosphere. \n\nSurface high pressure builds eastward into the area tonight, with \nwinds becoming light and skies staying mostly clear to partly \ncloudy. Not a perfect radiational cooling night it appears, as there \nare indications of SCT-BKN mid/high clouds late tonight into Sunday \nmorning, but still rather cool nonetheless as lows dip into the low \nto mid 40s. Would not be surprised if some locations sneak into the \nupper 30s. This high generally stays in place over the area on \nSunday, with mostly sunny skies, fairly light winds, and highs in \nthe 60s to near 70 degrees.\n\nLooking ahead to the upcoming week and next weekend, the overall \npattern looks mostly dry and mild. Grand Ensemble suggests the mid \nlevel flow remains from the northwest, with a lack of any \nsignificant shortwave troughs. It's tough to find any impactful \nweather this coming week, with the main highlight being the mild and \nabove normal temperatures. 850mb temps throughout the period are in \nthe 75th-90th percentile at times. Looking for highs in the 60s and \n70s, with temps flirting with 80 degrees Monday and Tuesday, then \nagain Friday and Saturday. One must pay attention to humidity values \nthis time of year as we creep closer to the fall fire season, but a \nlook through the week shows we are well above critical values for \nmuch of the week.",
